--TEST--
`void` and `never` are refused as a binding's declared type, and `var` is refused the type of a call that hands nothing back — `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/void-never-self-static
// `rule:types/grammar`: "`void` and `never` are return-only." A binding is
// neither, and until this landed both spellings type-checked: the written one
// declared a name for a value that does not exist, and the inferred one
// defined that name with nothing at all, which `nvs-ir` then reported as an
// operand used before it is defined.
final class Hands {
    public static function nothing(): void {}
}

void $written = Hands::nothing();

never $empty;

var $inferred = Hands::nothing();

// The call is fine on a line of its own. What is refused is giving a name to
// what it hands back, because it hands back nothing.
Hands::nothing();
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0742]: `void` is a return type only, and this is a binding
   --> case.nvs:12:1
%A
error[E0742]: `never` is a return type only, and this is a binding
   --> case.nvs:14:1
%A
error[E0742]: `void` is a return type only, and this is a binding
   --> case.nvs:16:17
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
